1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,362
Welcome to Module Twenty-One.

2
00:00:02,362 --> 00:00:06,525
Yesterday's forager could only find food by an exact landmark.

3
00:00:06,525 --> 00:00:08,687
Many animals do better than that.

4
00:00:08,687 --> 00:00:13,675
A shearwater flying over open ocean can't see the krill beneath the surface.

5
00:00:13,675 --> 00:00:20,650
But it can smell the plume of dimethyl sulphide that rises where plankton are being grazed, and it flies up the gradient.

6
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:22,925
It doesn't need an exact match.

7
00:00:22,925 --> 00:00:27,175
It needs a signal that grows stronger as it gets closer to what it wants.

8
00:00:27,175 --> 00:00:30,187
Embeddings give search that same kind of sense.

9
00:00:30,187 --> 00:00:31,550
Here's the idea.

10
00:00:31,550 --> 00:00:37,450
A small model turns every chunk of text into a point in a space with hundreds of dimensions.

11
00:00:37,450 --> 00:00:42,387
It places those points so that passages with similar meanings end up close together.

12
00:00:42,387 --> 00:00:44,500
Your question becomes a point too.

13
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:50,537
Searching then means finding the nearest chunks, even if they don't share a single word with your question.

14
00:00:50,537 --> 00:00:56,900
Ask about an agent forgetting its earlier rules, and the passage about compaction can finally turn up.

15
00:00:56,900 --> 00:00:58,937
These models can be small.

16
00:00:58,937 --> 00:01:03,800
One announced this month runs on a laptop in about half a gigabyte of memory.

17
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:10,550
One practical warning from today's sources: choose your embedding model before you build the index, and stick with it.

18
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:13,775
Points made by one model mean nothing to another.

19
00:01:13,775 --> 00:01:17,200
Switch models, and you have to re-embed everything.

20
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:19,950
So why not use embeddings alone?

21
00:01:19,950 --> 00:01:22,550
Because each sense has blind spots.

22
00:01:22,550 --> 00:01:25,175
Keyword search can't see synonyms.

23
00:01:25,175 --> 00:01:29,787
Embeddings can blur exact names, numbers and identifiers.

24
00:01:29,787 --> 00:01:36,062
And they find passages that are about the same topic, which is not the same as passages that answer your question.

25
00:01:36,062 --> 00:01:39,612
The answer most production systems use is hybrid search.

26
00:01:39,612 --> 00:01:45,775
Run both searches, and combine the two ranked lists with a simple method called reciprocal rank fusion.

27
00:01:45,775 --> 00:01:52,500
It ignores the raw scores, which aren't comparable, and looks only at where each passage ranked in each list.

28
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:55,762
Something near the top of both lists rises to the top.

29
00:01:55,762 --> 00:01:58,462
Here's where the smell metaphor breaks.

30
00:01:58,462 --> 00:02:01,762
A scent gradient is anchored in real chemistry.

31
00:02:01,762 --> 00:02:04,337
The plume really is stronger near the food.

32
00:02:04,337 --> 00:02:07,850
Embedding similarity is learned from patterns in text.

33
00:02:07,850 --> 00:02:12,425
Near means written about in similar ways, not true, and not useful.

34
00:02:12,425 --> 00:02:19,287
A fluent passage full of the right words can sit closer to your question than the dry paragraph that actually has the answer.

35
00:02:19,287 --> 00:02:21,362
The only way to know is to check.

36
00:02:21,362 --> 00:02:22,775
So that's the lab.

37
00:02:22,775 --> 00:02:29,375
You'll install a small, free embedding model, embed the course's source pack, and build a hybrid search.

38
00:02:29,375 --> 00:02:37,950
Then you'll run yesterday's ten labelled questions through three searches, keywords, embeddings and the hybrid, and compare their precision at five.

39
00:02:37,950 --> 00:02:41,400
Look question by question, not just at the average.

40
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:44,312
That's where you'll see each sense earning its keep.

41
00:02:44,312 --> 00:02:46,350
Let's go and follow the scent.

